# Routing for Netlify AND for Cloudflare Pages.
#
# ── The rewrite target has NO .html extension, and that is load-bearing. ─────
# Cloudflare Pages evaluates these rules BEFORE Pages Functions and then
# canonicalises an .html target to its pretty path. A rule pointing at
# "/app-shell.html" therefore did not serve the shell at all: it returned a
# bodyless 307 to /app-shell, functions/_middleware.ts never ran, and the
# browser received a document with no <script> at all. That is what made every
# private deep link a blank white page with zero JS requests. Verified with
# `wrangler pages dev dist` — see .github/scripts/test-pages-routing.mjs.
# Pointing at "/app-shell" instead serves the real 10 KB shell body directly.
#
# Both hosts resolve "/app-shell" to app-shell.html by the same pretty-URL
# lookup that serves every prerendered public route, so the target is portable.
#
# scripts/lib/private-routing.mjs is the shared source of truth for which
# prefixes are private, and .github/scripts/test-pages-routing.mjs asserts that
# this file still agrees with it.
#
# First matching rule wins, and a rule is only consulted when no
# static file matches the request — so the prerendered public pages written by
# scripts/prerender.mjs (dist/<route>/index.html) are served directly and never
# reach these rules.
#
# What is left for the rules to decide:
#
#   1. Private application surfaces -> the SPA shell, 200. They are client-routed
#      and guarded by ProtectedRoute / PlatformAdminRoute / PlatformOwnerRoute, so
#      the shell must still be served for deep links and page reloads.
#
#      The target is /app-shell, NOT /index.html: index.html is now the
#      prerendered HOMEPAGE, so falling back to it would serve marketing markup
#      (with the homepage canonical and an indexable robots tag) on the customer
#      portal and then discard it all on hydration. app-shell.html is the
#      untouched client template with an empty #root, marked noindex.
#
#      Both the exact prefix root AND its deep paths are listed: on Netlify
#      "/app/*" does NOT match "/app", and relying on the splat alone would 404
#      every portal root. Server-level noindex for these lives in public/_headers.
#
#   2. Everything else -> a real 404. Previously "/* /index.html 200" answered
#      every unknown URL with the homepage shell at HTTP 200, which turned the
#      entire unknown URL space into a soft 404.

/app                /app-shell     200
/app/*              /app-shell     200
/admin              /app-shell     200
/admin/*            /app-shell     200
/owner              /app-shell     200
/owner/*            /app-shell     200
/auth               /app-shell     200
/auth/*             /app-shell     200
/d                  /app-shell     200
/d/*                /app-shell     200

# @netlify-canonical-rewrites
# NETLIFY ONLY. When the build runs on Netlify (NETLIFY=true),
# scripts/prerender.mjs splices one rule per non-root public route in at this
# marker: "/route  /route.html  200!".
#
# Why forced, and why only there: a Netlify deployment of the <path>.html
# artifact answered cold requests to canonical URLs with ERR_TOO_MANY_REDIRECTS
# until those rewrites were added. The "!" makes the rewrite win over Netlify's
# own path normalisation. That syntax is Netlify-specific, so it is NEVER
# written into this committed file and never generated on Cloudflare or in a
# local build — Cloudflare's shared _redirects format takes numeric statuses
# only.
#
# No trailing-slash rules are generated. Netlify's documentation is explicit
# that redirects cannot reliably add or remove a trailing slash, and a forced
# "/route/ -> /route" rule can itself loop. "/route/" is therefore treated as a
# duplicate variant that carries the canonical "/route" in its <link rel=
# "canonical">, which is what consolidates it.

# NETLIFY ONLY IN PRACTICE. Cloudflare Pages rejects this rule outright —
# `wrangler pages dev` reports "Valid status codes are 200, 301, 302, 303, 307,
# or 308. Got 404." — and drops it. On Pages an unknown URL therefore falls
# through to functions/_middleware.ts, which serves /404 with a real 404 status.
# The rule is kept because it IS honoured on Netlify.
/*                  /404.html         404
